  "nameStory": "The Bark Mycena gets its straightforward name from its preferred growing spot - directly on tree bark. Unlike many of its bonnet cousins that prefer fallen logs or soil, this little mushroom has mastered the art of sprouting right from the rough bark surface of living and dead trees.",
  "description": "Bark Mycena is a delicate, small mushroom that looks like a tiny umbrella perched on tree bark. These dainty fungi typically measure just 1/2 to 1 inch across the cap, with slender stems that can reach 1-2 inches tall. They often appear in small groups, creating charming clusters of miniature mushrooms that seem to emerge directly from the bark's crevices and rough surfaces.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Growing Location",
      "description": "Grows directly on bark of trees, both living and dead - not on fallen logs or soil like most other small mushrooms",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Small whitish to pale gray cap, 1/2 to 1 inch across, bell-shaped when young, flattening with age and often developing a small central bump",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "White to pale grayish gills that attach to the stem, spaced moderately apart with a clean, papery appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Thin, fragile stem that's pale at the top and darker toward the base, often with tiny hairs visible under close inspection",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smell",
      "description": "Mild, slightly earthy odor - not particularly distinctive or strong when crushed",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Bark Mycena on the bark of both hardwood and conifer trees throughout New England's forests. They particularly favor oak, maple, and birch trees, appearing on both living trees and recently dead ones. You'll find them in mixed woodlands, along forest edges, and even on yard trees in suburban areas where mature trees are present.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Not typically fruiting yet, though mycelium may be developing within bark crevices as temperatures warm.",
    "summer": "Prime fruiting season begins in mid to late summer, especially after periods of warm, humid weather and gentle rains.",
    "fall": "Peak season continues through early fall, with the most abundant flushes occurring during September and October's moist conditions.",
    "winter": "Fruiting bodies disappear with cold weather, though the fungus persists within the bark substrate until next year."
  },
  "funFact": "Bark Mycena can actually help keep trees healthier by breaking down dead bark tissue and preventing harmful bacteria from penetrating deeper into the tree - they're like tiny bark janitors!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most easily confused with other small white mushrooms, but the key difference is location - if it's not growing directly on bark, it's not Bark Mycena. Other small white mushrooms typically grow on fallen logs, soil, or leaf litter.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The word 'mycena' comes from Greek meaning 'mushroom,' while 'corticola' means 'bark-dwelling' - so the scientific name literally translates to 'bark-dwelling mushroom,' making it one of the most accurately descriptive fungal names you'll encounter.",
  "compareHints": "Unlike Common Bonnet which grows on fallen logs and has a more brownish cap, or Milking Bonnet which releases white latex when broken, Bark Mycena stays distinctly pale and grows exclusively on standing bark. It's also smaller and more delicate than most of its bonnet relatives, rarely exceeding an inch in cap diameter.",
